# Probes scheduled by the strict-corpus gate on the 25-windows key ONLY.
#
# Read by scripts/jdk-only-strict-probes.sh, which appends these to its
# three-probe default. The file is keyed exactly like the .txt baseline beside
# it, because the ratchet is a set difference against that baseline: a probe
# scheduled on a key whose baseline was frozen without it turns every one of
# its divergent sections into a NEW row, and the gate fails. Adding a probe to
# the script's own default list would do that on every leg at once, which is
# why 104 of the 107 files in apps/probes/ were scheduled by nothing.
#
# THE BAR FOR A LINE HERE: the probe's three arms (HotSpot control, --real-jdk,
# --jdk-only) must all exit 0 AND all three transcripts must be byte-identical,
# on THIS key, on at least two separate runs. A probe that diverges is not
# barred from the gate -- it belongs in the .txt baseline instead, adjudicated
# section by section. What is barred is promoting one on a single observation:
# a probe seen clean once is a probe seen once.
#
# EVIDENCE. Windows 11, Temurin 25.0.3+9 -- the same image as the oracle.
# FOUR runs, all 2026-09-08, across two different binaries and both build
# profiles:
#   run 1  05:17-06:05  release. The sweep that first ran all 107 files, each
#                       probe in its own harness invocation. Verdict CLEAN =
#                       every arm exit 0 and both modes byte-identical to the
#                       control. 62 of the 107 came back CLEAN.
#   run 2  ~10:00       release, freshly built from dev @ 2b0913374, all of
#                       them in ONE invocation through this file: 69 probes,
#                       207 arms, every one exit 0, ZERO divergent sections
#                       contributed by any promoted probe. PASS.
#   run 3  ~10:30       the four IO families re-run (they came from the IO
#                       sweep, not the 107-probe one), plus the discovery that
#                       their fifth sibling L4FilesSweep is NOT clean.
#   run 4  ~11:15       DEBUG binary -- what ci.yml's step actually runs.
#                       Same verdict. An optimisation-dependent "clean" would
#                       have shown up here and did not.
#
# All four are the same day, so this is repetition, not soak. It is the bar the
# file states and no more: enough that a probe is not clean by luck of a single
# run, not enough to call any of them proven stable. A line that turns out to
# flake belongs in prose like the FLAKY block of tools/e2e-ratchet.txt, deleted
# from here -- never left in to redden the leg at random.
#
# 25435 rows of transcript across the 64 are now checked against a HotSpot
# control that nothing checked them against before.
#
# COST, measured on the DEBUG binary because that is the one CI builds:
#     3 probes (this file absent)   145s
#    67 probes (this file present)  876s     -> +731s on the blocking leg
# A release binary is far cheaper -- the 69-probe set (these 64 plus the two
# dropped below) took 345s -- but CI does not build one for this step, so 731s
# is the number to argue with. If it ever stops being worth it, cut lines from
# here: the file is a list, and shortening it cannot make the gate wrong.
#
# FOUR MORE, from the IO sweep rather than the 107-probe one, same bar:
#   run 1  2026-09-07  the-io-probe-families-swept-on-windows-for-the-first-time
#   run 2  2026-09-08  re-run here; hs/real/strict all 0, zero divergent
#                      sections, 1347 further rows
#     AsyncChannelSweep FilePathSweep IoSystemSweep L4FileSweep
# Their fifth sibling L4FilesSweep is NOT here: the same re-run found it
# diverging in BOTH modes over 10 sections, which is a lead, not a promotion --
# the-files-sweep-that-could-not-run-until-yesterday-found-four-nio-defects-20260908.md
#
# TWO THAT PASSED THE BAR AND ARE STILL NOT HERE, on cost. Measured on the
# DEBUG binary, which is what ci.yml's step actually runs:
#     FjpDeepSum      564s to check    1 row
#     InvokeAllCount  436s to check    7 rows
# Between them 1000s of a 1784s run, for 8 rows. Both are 100k-iteration
# concurrency workloads that print a handful of summary counts, so almost all
# of that time is the workload and almost none of it is the check. They are
# clean -- they were clean in all three runs -- and they can come back the day
# something wants a long-running concurrency arm. A transcript-diff gate is
# not that thing. `ChmKeySetGrowth` is the same shape at 39s and is kept: the
# line being drawn here is cost, not shape.
#
# What is lost by dropping them, said plainly: ExecutorService.invokeAll over
# 100k tasks is covered by nothing else in this file, and deep ForkJoin
# recursion only by `FjpProbe` (3 rows, 11s) and `ForkJoinShadowSweep`
# (178 rows, 18s), which do not go 200k deep.
#
# NOT here, and each for a reason worth keeping:
#   * the 30 BOTH / 5 REAL-ONLY / 2 STRICT-ONLY divergent probes -- they are
#     leads, and four of them are written up in
#     phase-2s-last-lead-closed-itself-and-the-sweep-found-five-that-retirement-cannot-take-20260908.md
#   * the 6 whose HotSpot control exits non-zero -- an oracle that fails is not
#     an oracle, and a gate cannot adjudicate what it cannot measure
#   * the 2 whose CratonVM arms exit non-zero
#   * NrpVariants -- its rows are JIT-timing dependent (see
#     invokespecial-on-a-null-receiver-lost-its-npe-again-...-20260908.md), so
#     it would be red at random, which is the one thing a ratchet must not be
